  k=13: 1/(2k)=1/26  1/(k+1)=1/14  gap_to_conjecture=3/91  Tao_shape_unit(c=1)~0.017106  Bedert_shape_unit(c=1)~0.013913
  k=16: 1/(2k)=1/32  1/(k+1)=1/17  gap_to_conjecture=15/544  Tao_shape_unit(c=1)~0.010414  Bedert_shape_unit(c=1)~0.009843
  k=17: 1/(2k)=1/34  1/(k+1)=1/18  gap_to_conjecture=4/153  Tao_shape_unit(c=1)~0.009039  Bedert_shape_unit(c=1)~0.008897
  The constants in the global theorems are not asserted here; the point is
  that a recursive sieve certificate at 1/(k+1) is far above the Tao regime
  on any instance where it applies.

4. Tournament Analysis
  vertices: speed-set proof obligations
  observable: best certified margin, first conjecture-reaching tier, denominator
  switch: larger margin wins; ties prefer earlier tier and smaller q
  fingerprints: {'vertices': ['AP_k13', 'noncovered_random_k13', 'blind_lcm_2_14', 'blind_lcm_2_24', 'S562_packet_n14', 'S562_packet_n14_lift', 'S562_packet_n17', 'S562_packet_n18'], 'score_hist': {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1, 7: 1}, 'directed_3_cycles': 0, 'sccs': [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1], 'hamiltonian_paths': 1}

5. Synthesis
  Coarse THM-369 already proves the full 1/(k+1) bound off the sieve-covered core.
  The multi-sieve tiers are useful precisely inside that core:
    blind_lcm_* has no q<=14 witness but gets t=2/27 with margin 2/27.
    S562 n=14 residual packets get fine-window witnesses such as 6/23.
    S562 n=17 residual packets get a dyadic-residual witness 9/25.
  This does not beat Tao as a universal asymptotic theorem; it shrinks the
  Tao/Bedert hard regime to a recursively generated near-AP residual core.
